{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01PbQ55WeusUmEcZMsztrFr7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015Qe2PUrzSFxdtjvWdeypG5","name":"mcp__workspace__bash","input":{"command":"
SIM_DIR="/sessions/local_c63pr3qsd5/mnt/outputs/artifacts/ic-sim-cadence"
RUN_ID="20260623T204928Z"

cat > "$SIM_DIR/discussion.json" << 'DISCUSSION_EOF'
{
  "assessment_mode": "sub-agent",
  "assessment_mode_intentional": true,
  "partner_verdicts": [
    {
      "partner": "visionary",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "The market timing is right — SMB field service digitization is real and accelerating. But the vision is too narrow. 'Scheduling and dispatch' is a feature, not a platform. Need to hear a compounding 10-year thesis before committing, and the portfolio conflict situation must be resolved at the IC level."
    },
    {
      "partner": "operator",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Traction is real — 120 paying customers and 18% MoM growth is not noise. But GTM is a black box, churn data is absent, and neither founder has domain experience in field service trades. I need to see the acquisition playbook and cohort retention before I can say this scales."
    },
    {
      "partner": "analyst",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "The unit economics surface well — 4.5-month CAC payback and 80% gross margins are exceptional. But without churn data, the LTV/CAC calculation is directional at best. The $16M pre at 33x ARR is elevated. Need cohort curves, a 24-month financial model, and a valuation sanity check before this is underwritable."
    }
  ],
  "debate_sections": [
    {
      "topic": "Opening — The Traction Signal",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Let me start with what has me excited about this deal. Cadence has hit 18% MoM growth for four consecutive months. That is not a rounding error — that's a company accelerating into real demand. The field service sector is having its Shopify moment: millions of HVAC techs and plumbers are still running on whiteboards and phone calls. The ex-Stripe, ex-Notion team has built a product that is clearly resonating. I want to talk about what this could become."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I share the enthusiasm on the headline growth, but I want to be precise about what we actually know. We know 120 businesses paid for this product. We do not know how they found it, whether they are still paying, or whether the growth rate is founders personally calling every prospect. At 18% MoM with no GTM disclosure, this could be a great product or a founder-led hustle that plateaus at 200 customers. I cannot tell yet."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The operator is right to be precise. Here is what the numbers do tell us: $1,200 CAC against $333 ARPU at 80% gross margins gives you a 4.5-month payback. That is genuinely exceptional — top-decile for B2B SaaS at seed. What the numbers do not tell us is whether customers stay. If monthly churn is 5%, LTV/CAC is still around 4x. If it is 10%, you are at 2x and the investment thesis is structurally weak. We are debating a company where the most important number — churn — is not in front of us."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Portfolio Conflict — The Room in the Room",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "Before we go further, I want to put the conflict situation on the table. The conflict check came back with four blocking conflicts — DispatchPro, HouseCall Pro, Jobber, and Kickserv — all direct competitors in the SMB field service scheduling space. That is before you count WorkWave and ServiceTitan, which are manageable but still in the same category. The evaluation criteria are explicit: a blocking portfolio conflict is a dealbreaker dimension that forces a hard pass regardless of score. Are we sure we want to proceed past this point?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I hear you on the conflict check output, but I want to push back on the mechanistic application here. The question is whether Cadence represents a genuinely different wedge in this market, not just whether the same sector words appear in multiple portfolio company descriptions. ServiceTitan is enterprise. HouseCall Pro and Jobber are both well-established mid-market players. If Cadence is building the next-generation SMB product with a PLG motion and modern UX, that is a different bet, not a competing bet. The partnership needs to decide whether we believe there is a distinct category here."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I agree with the visionary that sector overlap alone is not always a dealbreaker — we have invested in multiple CRM companies before. But the specific overlap here is not just sector, it is product category and customer segment. DispatchPro and Kickserv are literally SMB field service scheduling platforms. HouseCall Pro serves home services SMBs with scheduling and dispatch. Jobber has 200,000 SMB field service customers. The question is whether we can make a credible argument to our LPs and to Cadence's future investors that we are not holding competing interests. Tactically, this may mean we need to resolve or exit positions before committing to Cadence, or find a co-investor structure that provides cover."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The operator has named the right framing. This is not just an ethical question — it is a fiduciary one. If Cadence competes directly with our Jobber position and we invest in Cadence, we have a conflict of interest that must be disclosed and managed. My recommendation is we park this as a conditional diligence requirement: before any term sheet discussion, the partnership must formally map Cadence's ICP and GTM against each of the four flagged portfolio companies and get sign-off from fund counsel. If there is a genuine wedge, let us document it. If there is not, this is a pass regardless of the traction."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Team — Domain Expertise Gap",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "The team concern I keep coming back to is domain expertise. Ex-Stripe and ex-Notion are impressive credentials — I would take those in most categories. But field service SMBs are one of the hardest customer segments to build for. The buying journey is often a single owner-operator who is also the head technician. The workflows are operationally specific: job dispatch has different logic than calendar scheduling, crew availability has dependencies on equipment and certifications, and invoicing is tied to job completion in ways that are non-obvious. Have these founders actually spent time in a plumber's van? Do they understand what makes a dispatcher's day hard?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I would counter that outsider perspective is sometimes the advantage. Jobber was built by founders who were not plumbers — they were software engineers who were frustrated by the existing tools. The fact that neither of these founders comes from the trades means they have not been captured by the industry's assumptions about how the workflow has to work. The ex-Notion PM in particular knows how to build tools that workers actually want to use every day, not tools that IT departments want to buy. That is a legitimate edge in a market where the incumbents have over-engineered for enterprise and neglected the SMB UX."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "I think both of you are right, which means the answer is in the customer evidence. If the founders can show us that their 120 customers are deeply activated — high login frequency, high feature utilization, low early churn, NPS that is meaningfully above category average — then the outsider advantage theory holds. If the retention curves are choppy, the domain gap is the explanation. We should not resolve this debate with priors about founder backgrounds; we should resolve it with customer data."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Vision — Scheduling Tool vs. Platform",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Here is my core hesitation. I can get to a $50M ARR story for Cadence with the current product. Better scheduling UX, PLG motion, captures the SMB segment that Jobber and ServiceTitan have neglected. But I cannot get to a $500M ARR story unless the founders have a clear vision for what comes after scheduling. The category leaders in vertical SaaS — Toast for restaurants, Veeva for pharma, ServiceTitan at the top of field service — all became platforms. They expanded from the scheduling wedge into payments, financing, inventory, compliance. Do Cadence's founders know which adjacent layer they own next, and why?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "The platform question is real, but I think it is premature for a company at $480K ARR. The companies you are citing — Toast, ServiceTitan — did not articulate their platform vision at seed. They got their core product right first. What I want to know right now is whether the founders have figured out the scheduling product deeply enough to win at that layer, because you cannot build a platform on a product that customers are churning out of. Let us not demand a 10-year roadmap before we have seen a 12-month cohort."
        },
