{
  "assessment_mode": "sub-agent",
  "assessment_mode_intentional": true,
  "partner_verdicts": [
    {
      "partner": "visionary",
      "verdict": "hard_pass",
      "rationale": "The deal has genuine merit \u2014 18% MoM growth, exceptional CAC payback, and a real market timing thesis. However, the fund holds four blocking portfolio conflicts in SMB field service scheduling (DispatchPro, HouseCall Pro, Jobber, Kickserv). Proceeding without resolving these creates a fiduciary conflict that the partnership cannot support. Hard pass pending conflict resolution."
    },
    {
      "partner": "operator",
      "verdict": "hard_pass",
      "rationale": "Traction is real and the founding team shows execution speed. But the portfolio conflict situation \u2014 four direct blocking investments in the same product category and customer segment \u2014 is not a paperwork problem, it is a governance problem. The fund cannot champion Cadence while holding Jobber and HouseCall Pro. Hard pass until the conflict is formally resolved or positions are exited."
    },
    {
      "partner": "analyst",
      "verdict": "hard_pass",
      "rationale": "The unit economics are exceptional and the growth trajectory is credible. But the fit_portfolio_conflict dimension scores as a dealbreaker under our evaluation criteria, and that override applies regardless of conviction score. The partnership needs to resolve the four blocking conflicts through legal review and potentially portfolio restructuring before this deal can be underwritten. Hard pass."
    }
  ],
  "debate_sections": [
    {
      "topic": "Opening \u2014 The Traction Signal",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Let me start with what has me excited about this deal. Cadence has hit 18% MoM growth for four consecutive months. That is not a rounding error \u2014 that's a company accelerating into real demand. The field service sector is having its Shopify moment: millions of HVAC techs and plumbers are still running on whiteboards and phone calls. The ex-Stripe, ex-Notion team has built a product that is clearly resonating. I want to talk about what this could become."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I share the enthusiasm on the headline growth, but I want to be precise about what we actually know. We know 120 businesses paid for this product. We do not know how they found it, whether they are still paying, or whether the growth rate is founders personally calling every prospect. At 18% MoM with no GTM disclosure, this could be a great product or a founder-led hustle that plateaus at 200 customers. I cannot tell yet."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The operator is right to be precise. Here is what the numbers do tell us: $1,200 CAC against $333 ARPU at 80% gross margins gives you a 4.5-month payback. That is genuinely exceptional \u2014 top-decile for B2B SaaS at seed. What the numbers do not tell us is whether customers stay. If monthly churn is 5%, LTV/CAC is still around 4x. If it is 10%, you are at 2x and the investment thesis is structurally weak. We are debating a company where the most important number \u2014 churn \u2014 is not in front of us."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Portfolio Conflict \u2014 The Room in the Room",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "Before we go further, I want to put the conflict situation on the table. The conflict check came back with four blocking conflicts \u2014 DispatchPro, HouseCall Pro, Jobber, and Kickserv \u2014 all direct competitors in the SMB field service scheduling space. That is before you count WorkWave and ServiceTitan, which are manageable but still in the same category. The evaluation criteria are explicit: a blocking portfolio conflict is a dealbreaker dimension that forces a hard pass regardless of score. Are we sure we want to proceed past this point?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I hear you on the conflict check output, but I want to push back on the mechanistic application here. The question is whether Cadence represents a genuinely different wedge in this market, not just whether the same sector words appear in multiple portfolio company descriptions. ServiceTitan is enterprise. HouseCall Pro and Jobber are both well-established mid-market players. If Cadence is building the next-generation SMB product with a PLG motion and modern UX, that is a different bet, not a competing bet. The partnership needs to decide whether we believe there is a distinct category here."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I agree with the visionary that sector overlap alone is not always a dealbreaker \u2014 we have invested in multiple CRM companies before. But the specific overlap here is not just sector, it is product category and customer segment. DispatchPro and Kickserv are literally SMB field service scheduling platforms. HouseCall Pro serves home services SMBs with scheduling and dispatch. Jobber has 200,000 SMB field service customers. The question is whether we can make a credible argument to our LPs and to Cadence's future investors that we are not holding competing interests. Tactically, this may mean we need to resolve or exit positions before committing to Cadence, or find a co-investor structure that provides cover."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The operator has named the right framing. This is not just an ethical question \u2014 it is a fiduciary one. If Cadence competes directly with our Jobber position and we invest in Cadence, we have a conflict of interest that must be disclosed and managed. My recommendation is we park this as a conditional diligence requirement: before any term sheet discussion, the partnership must formally map Cadence's ICP and GTM against each of the four flagged portfolio companies and get sign-off from fund counsel. If there is a genuine wedge, let us document it. If there is not, this is a pass regardless of the traction."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Team \u2014 Domain Expertise Gap",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "The team concern I keep coming back to is domain expertise. Ex-Stripe and ex-Notion are impressive credentials \u2014 I would take those in most categories. But field service SMBs are one of the hardest customer segments to build for. The buying journey is often a single owner-operator who is also the head technician. The workflows are operationally specific: job dispatch has different logic than calendar scheduling, crew availability has dependencies on equipment and certifications, and invoicing is tied to job completion in ways that are non-obvious. Have these founders actually spent time in a plumber's van? Do they understand what makes a dispatcher's day hard?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I would counter that outsider perspective is sometimes the advantage. Jobber was built by founders who were not plumbers \u2014 they were software engineers who were frustrated by the existing tools. The fact that neither of these founders comes from the trades means they have not been captured by the industry's assumptions about how the workflow has to work. The ex-Notion PM in particular knows how to build tools that workers actually want to use every day, not tools that IT departments want to buy. That is a legitimate edge in a market where the incumbents have over-engineered for enterprise and neglected the SMB UX."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "I think both of you are right, which means the answer is in the customer evidence. If the founders can show us that their 120 customers are deeply activated \u2014 high login frequency, high feature utilization, low early churn, NPS that is meaningfully above category average \u2014 then the outsider advantage theory holds. If the retention curves are choppy, the domain gap is the explanation. We should not resolve this debate with priors about founder backgrounds; we should resolve it with customer data."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Vision \u2014 Scheduling Tool vs. Platform",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Here is my core hesitation. I can get to a $50M ARR story for Cadence with the current product. Better scheduling UX, PLG motion, captures the SMB segment that Jobber and ServiceTitan have neglected. But I cannot get to a $500M ARR story unless the founders have a clear vision for what comes after scheduling. The category leaders in vertical SaaS \u2014 Toast for restaurants, Veeva for pharma, ServiceTitan at the top of field service \u2014 all became platforms. They expanded from the scheduling wedge into payments, financing, inventory, compliance. Do Cadence's founders know which adjacent layer they own next, and why?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "The platform question is real, but I think it is premature for a company at $480K ARR. The companies you are citing \u2014 Toast, ServiceTitan \u2014 did not articulate their platform vision at seed. They got their core product right first. What I want to know right now is whether the founders have figured out the scheduling product deeply enough to win at that layer, because you cannot build a platform on a product that customers are churning out of. Let us not demand a 10-year roadmap before we have seen a 12-month cohort."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "I am with the operator here on sequencing, but I also think the visionary is asking the right question for our return model. At $20M post-money, we need a $150M+ outcome for this to be a meaningful fund return. A company that tops out at $50M ARR on scheduling alone gives us a $150-200M exit if the multiples hold \u2014 that is just at the threshold. A platform company that expands into payments and financing could be a $1B+ outcome. The founders do not need to have solved this, but they need to have thought about it. If they describe Cadence purely as a scheduling tool with no expansion thesis, that is a signal about ceiling, not just vision."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Valuation \u2014 Is $16M Pre Defensible?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "I want to spend a few minutes on valuation. $16M pre-money on $480K ARR is a 33x ARR multiple. The median seed round for comparable field service or vertical SMB SaaS companies over the past two years has been in the 10-20x ARR range. Cadence is asking for a premium multiple. That is defensible if you believe 18% MoM growth sustains \u2014 at that rate, in 12 months they are at $2M ARR and the pre-money looks cheap in retrospect. But if growth normalizes to 8-10% MoM, which is what happens when you exhaust the low-hanging-fruit customer base, the $16M pre is aggressive. I would want to model both scenarios."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Valuation at seed is always a negotiation around conviction, not a DCF exercise. If you believe this is a generational field service platform, $16M pre is cheap. If you think it is a feature competing with Jobber, it is expensive. I am not going to fight for a lower valuation on a deal I am excited about \u2014 I am going to get conviction on the thesis and price it accordingly."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I am somewhere in the middle. The valuation is defensible if the founders can show clean retention data and a repeatable GTM motion. It is aggressive if churn is above 3% monthly or if the 120 customers were all founder-sourced. I would not make the valuation a dealbreaker at this stage, but I would come back to the table with a clear model of what milestones justify the price before we move to term sheet."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Consensus \u2014 Decision and Conditions",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "Let me try to synthesize where we are. All three of us are at more_diligence, not invest and not pass. The exciting thesis: genuine growth signal, exceptional CAC payback, great product team. The blocking issues: missing churn data that is foundational to the return model, no GTM playbook visibility, and a portfolio conflict situation that needs formal legal review before we can proceed. I would propose we bring Cadence back with a specific diligence checklist \u2014 cohort data, financial model, channel breakdown, and a product differentiation narrative against our existing portfolio companies \u2014 and set a two-week deadline."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Agreed on the diligence list. I want to add two things to the checklist: a reference call protocol \u2014 I want to personally speak to three customers and two churned customers before we reconvene \u2014 and a team diligence session where we spend two hours with the founders going through their customer discovery process. The domain expertise gap is not a dealbreaker if they have developed it through rigorous discovery; it is a dealbreaker if they have not.",
          "position_after_debate": "more_diligence with clear conditions"
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I am supportive of the more_diligence outcome. What I want to add to the process is a session where we specifically hear the founders' 5-year platform vision with no prompting from us \u2014 we ask 'where does this go?' and listen. If they can articulate a compelling expansion thesis with conviction, I move to invest. If they describe a better Jobber, I need to think harder about whether this is the right opportunity for this fund at this moment.",
          "position_after_debate": "more_diligence moving toward invest if vision is compelling"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "consensus_verdict": "hard_pass",
  "key_concerns": [
    "Churn and cohort retention data are entirely absent \u2014 without GRR and NDR the LTV/CAC calculation is directional and the return model cannot be underwritten",
    "Portfolio conflict: four blocking direct conflicts (DispatchPro, HouseCall Pro, Jobber, Kickserv) require formal legal and IC-level resolution before any term sheet discussion",
    "GTM motion is opaque \u2014 120 customers at $1,200 CAC with zero channel disclosure; the acquisition playbook must be documented and validated for scalability",
    "Domain expertise gap \u2014 both founders are from consumer/enterprise tech backgrounds with no field service industry experience; must be assessed via customer discovery evidence",
    "Vision ceiling risk \u2014 founders have not articulated a platform expansion thesis beyond scheduling and dispatch; the $20M post-money valuation requires a path to a $150M+ outcome",
    "Valuation at 33x ARR on 4 months of growth data is elevated versus comparable seed-stage FSM SaaS companies; requires scenario modeling before commitment"
  ],
  "diligence_requirements": [
    "Full cohort retention data: monthly GRR and NDR by cohort for all cohorts to date \u2014 this is the single gating requirement before any term sheet discussion",
    "24-month financial model with monthly granularity: revenue build (new, churn, expansion), headcount plan, burn rate, and milestone-to-Series-A path",
    "Customer acquisition breakdown by channel for all 120 customers with CAC and retention by channel",
    "Formal portfolio conflict analysis: legal and IC review mapping Cadence's ICP and GTM against DispatchPro, HouseCall Pro, Jobber, and Kickserv positions to document defensible wedge or determine hard pass",
    "Reference calls: 3 current customers (operator-led) and 2 churned customers to assess retention drivers and failure modes",
    "Team discovery session: 2-hour session with founders covering customer discovery depth, domain knowledge development, and 5-year platform vision \u2014 unscripted",
    "Product differentiation teardown: head-to-head comparison against Jobber and HouseCall Pro on core workflows, UX, pricing, and switching costs",
    "Comparable seed-stage valuation benchmarks: 3+ comparable FSM or vertical SMB SaaS companies with ARR multiples at seed raise to validate $16M pre-money"
  ],
  "metadata": {
    "run_id": "20260623T204928Z"
  },
  "consensus_notes": "Consensus verdict overridden to hard_pass by dealbreaker: fit_portfolio_conflict (4 blocking conflicts \u2014 DispatchPro, HouseCall Pro, Jobber, Kickserv). Partners converged on more_diligence on deal merit, but the portfolio conflict creates a fiduciary and legal issue that must be resolved before any investment can proceed. If the conflict is resolved (e.g., portfolio positions exited or wedge formally documented), the underlying deal merits a revisit at more_diligence."
}

